#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120690} :activity/id #uuid "711b406b-35cb-5fdc-b1b0-d63789565aba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823 :topic/id #uuid "c321a6fc-a622-5c4d-89a5-d91936b9920c" :topic/math-academy-id 1856} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121041 :tutorial/content "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d09211935b438f1189322ded40683a1f18618e35f3e.svg)\n\nSo, the set ![](images/24/2449d6abf866e2baa123e81c1d610940930c75c6b45ecf818d36c62191ef605f.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "892648a7-3b42-5d92-9592-1a8859cc6b0c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6cd915cb-1be5-57c4-9174-958e51421e23" :step/math-academy-id 17508 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119627 :question/id #uuid "1c4e02bb-5ec0-572e-92ba-46d16c6f03fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433767}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f601bb80-6906-5220-b7c7-22b3ad93f773" :step/math-academy-id 17509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119628 :question/id #uuid "1cbcbca0-c8b5-55c9-af93-9b9c0ba59ffb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e66c269-233b-57f8-9dae-46eb78c228bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433764}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for a Given Null Space"} :ste